                <pb facs="0485" n="227" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg227"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1326</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)
                        <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-13">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 13, 1915</hi>.</date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 8.40 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2449. Your 1795, 1st instant.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d310-f1"/> Have presented
                    matter pursuant to Department’s 1750, 22d ultimo. In reply Foreign Office states
                    as follows:</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>In reply I have the honour to point out that such goods do not come under the
                        control of the prize court until after they have been discharged from the
                        ship conveying them and that there would therefore be no special advantage
                        in chartering a vessel to carry them as is suggested. When however, the
                        goods have been discharged and placed in the prize court, every facility
                        will be given to neutral owners to establish their title to the goods or to
                        the proceeds of their sale.</p>
                </quote>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
